1|1@television^1438928527#ctujjey:goodbetterbestbested$:It changed the country more than the assassination of JFK ever did. We were on a Cold War trajectory before and after his assassination, JFK had no real intention of changing that aspect of foreign policy, though he was virulently anti-communist. Only after 9/11 did the U.S turn away from its temporary rejection of Cold War hegemony and turn back to an interventionist foreign policy. The moral position of the U.S. in the world that it had gained after the fall of tyrannical and undemocratic Soviet-style governments, along with the unanimity around the world condemning the events on 9/11, was squandered with the invasion of Iraq.
1|0@television^1438939143#ctumcow:assessmentdeterred$:&gt;Only after 9/11 did the U.S turn away from its temporary rejection of Cold War hegemony and turn back to an interventionist foreign policy. Its not really important, but I don't think it's fair to say the US ever rejected the concept of pursuing hegemony. Rather, with the fall of the Soviet Union US power was undisputed, so there was no need to force it. 9/11 just awoke the beast from it's slumber, and it ended up attacking a country which in purely realist terms posed no legitimate threat to it's international power. 
0|0@television^1439011683#ctvo1o0:goodbetterbestbested$:Without force, what is hegemony? It is consensus. The fall of the Soviet Union and the freeing of people from tyrannical governments forged a consensus around the world that the United States bore some kind of better way of life, flawed as it was and is. The invasion of Iraq under reckless and unconfirmed (if not actively false) pretenses was the type of aggression that flipped that consensus into its opposite. It was probably among the worst foreign policy mistakes the country has ever made.
