7|0@television^1438930598#ctuk79g:MyNewNewUserName$:My son was born on 9/3/01 so I was a mess in so many ways on 9/11, 8 days post-partum, my entire family had invaded our house, and then I woke up on 9/11, nursed my baby, and watched America be attacked. I was 29. I knew this was our "JFK Moment" -- we will always know where we were when it happened. When we watched the towers fall on live TV I felt like we had 8 days of wonderful, tremendous hope, and then a terrible weight of fear and dread and uncertainty. This was not the world I had born my son into just a week before. We started renting stacks of comedic movies instead of watching the 24-7 TV coverage. It was relentless, It was heartbreaking. And it was on every channel. When we finally tuned in to media again, Jon Stewart reminded me to grieve but not despair. He was of my generation -- unlike Peter Jennings and Tom Brokaw and Dan Rather, who are my parent's age. He didn't have to pretend to connect with us. He was us, and he reflected and shaped our thoughts. He's truly irreplaceable, and he'll be missed. 
