62|0@television^1438915334#ctudllt:RealLiveGirl$:I've been looking for this for years. Somehow this episode was cemented in my brain. Not only due to the horrific events on 9/11, but Jon Stewart helped to mark this turning point in modern culture. He found a way to appeal to a specific generation. The same way art, music, drugs, etc. do during moments of significant upheaval. However, he radicalized a group of apathetic 90s kids into a political conversation that was not pandering, not contrived, but blunt, intelligent and catering to the inquisitive demographic. Jon encourages free thinking. He encourages involvement in the world around us. Sure, he's said some stupid shit. But that's what we love about him. He's human. BUT the best part of all is that owns up to it. Ever watch that ridiculous Tucker Carlson video from 2006? Fucking love this guy... The following is dedicated to Jon Stewart AND Stephen Colbert (and is no way taken from a quote from one of the bestest movies ever) "Some birds are not meant to be caged, that's all. Their feathers are too bright, their songs too sweet and wild. So you let them go, or when you open the cage to feed them they somehow fly out past you. And the part of you that knows it was wrong to imprison them in the first place rejoices, but still, the place where you live is that much more drab and empty for their departure."
